WEBVTT

1
. --> .
Before we touch the worked example, separate the three ideas that students often blur together.

2
. --> .
Accuracy asks whether a result is close to the accepted or true value.

3
. --> .
Precision asks whether repeated readings agree closely with one another.

4
. --> .
And trustworthiness asks a wider question: do the tool, the method, and the reported uncertainty justify the claim you are making.

5
. --> .
A set of readings can be precise but still wrong.

6
. --> .
A result can look close to the accepted value once, but still come from a weak method.

7
. --> .
So this lesson is about judging measurement quality with more than one lens.

8
. --> .
Start with the language of the lesson.

9
. --> .
Accuracy asks, how close is the result to the accepted value.

10
. --> .
Precision asks, how tightly do repeated readings cluster together.

11
. --> .
Trustworthiness asks, does the whole measurement story make sense, including the chosen instrument, repeat readings, possible errors, and stated uncertainty.

12
. --> .
That means you should not call a result excellent just because it has many digits or because one reading looks neat.

13
. --> .
You need to know which question you are answering first.

14
. --> .
Now use the lesson analogy.

15
. --> .
Imagine a target board.

16
. --> .
If the dart cluster lands near the centre, that suggests accuracy because the average result is close to the accepted value.

17
. --> .
If the darts land tightly together, that suggests precision because the readings agree closely with each other.

18
. --> .
A tight cluster far from the centre is precise but not accurate.

19
. --> .
A wide cluster near the centre can be accurate on average but not very precise.

20
. --> .
This picture gives you a fast visual test before you even touch the worked example.

21
. --> .
The lesson then lays out the common patterns explicitly.

22
. --> .
Accurate and precise means the readings are close to the accepted value and tightly grouped.

23
. --> .
Precise but not accurate means the readings agree with one another, but the whole cluster is shifted away from the accepted value.

24
. --> .
Accurate but not very precise means the readings are spread out, yet the average lands near the accepted value.

25
. --> .
And neither means the readings are scattered and also off target.

26
. --> .
Looking at only one of these ideas gives an incomplete judgement of the measurement quality.

27
. --> .
This is where systematic error matters.

28
. --> .
Suppose a balance always reads zero point two grams too high.

29
. --> .
Repeated measurements may cluster tightly, so the set can look precise.

30
. --> .
But the whole group is shifted in the same direction, which weakens the accuracy.

31
. --> .
That is why precision does not guarantee truth.

32
. --> .
A constant offset, a zero error, or a calibration problem can keep every reading neatly grouped while still pushing them away from the accepted value.

33
. --> .
Random error looks different.

34
. --> .
Instead of shifting every reading the same way, it makes the readings bounce above and below the best estimate.

35
. --> .
That spread tells you about precision.

36
. --> .
If the spread is wide, the precision is weaker.

37
. --> .
If the spread is narrow, the precision is stronger.

38
. --> .
This is why repeat readings matter so much.

39
. --> .
They let you see whether the method is stable or whether the values are wandering too much to inspire confidence.

40
. --> .
The lesson then widens the judgement to trustworthiness.

41
. --> .
Trust comes from choosing a suitable instrument, taking repeated readings when needed, watching for systematic and random error, and reporting a reasonable uncertainty instead of pretending to exactness.

42
. --> .
A result becomes stronger when the method and the numbers support each other.

43
. --> .
So a trustworthy measurement is not just a neat number on a screen.

44
. --> .
It is a number that has been earned by a sensible process.

45
. --> .
Honest reporting is part of trustworthiness too.

46
. --> .
A measurement should not pretend to be exact if the tool and method do not justify that claim.

47
. --> .
That is why uncertainty matters.

48
. --> .
And when you want to compare how large that uncertainty is relative to the measured value, the lesson gives you another tool: percentage uncertainty equals absolute uncertainty divided by measured value, then multiplied by one hundred percent.

49
. --> .
Even if the worked example in this lesson is not a percentage-uncertainty calculation, that formula strengthens the bigger idea that trustworthy measurement is about justified claims.

50
. --> .
Use two anchors to keep the lesson practical.

51
. --> .
First, imagine a tight cluster of readings well away from the accepted value.

52
. --> .
That set is precise but not accurate.

53
. --> .
Second, compare a coarse reading rounded to the nearest whole gram with a finer reading shown to the nearest tenth of a gram.

54
. --> .
The finer reading is more precise because it comes from a measurement with smaller resolution steps.

55
. --> .
Together, those two examples stop students from confusing neat agreement with truth.

56
. --> .
Pause and check your own thinking.

57
. --> .
If readings are tightly grouped but far from the accepted value, which idea is strong and which is weak?

58
. --> .
Precision is strong, but accuracy is weak.

59
. --> .
If readings are spread out, what part of measurement quality should you question first?

60
. --> .
The precision.

61
. --> .
And what helps make a result trustworthy besides the number itself?

62
. --> .
A suitable tool, sensible repeats, controlled errors, and honest uncertainty.

63
. --> .
If any one of those felt shaky, return to the target-board picture before moving on.

64
. --> .
You are now ready for the worked example in this lesson.

65
. --> .
The guided example asks which reading is more precise when two scales report the same object in different ways.

66
. --> .
Read it in a clean sequence.

67
. --> .
First, do not ask which reading seems more correct.

68
. --> .
Ask which one shows finer resolution.

69
. --> .
Second, notice that a reading reported to the nearest tenth of a gram carries more detail than one rounded to the nearest whole gram.

70
. --> .
Third, connect that finer scale step to precision, because precision is about how finely the measurement can distinguish values.

71
. --> .
So the finer tenth-gram reading is the more precise one.

72
. --> .
If that chain already feels clear, the worked example will feel like confirmation rather than a surprise.
